WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.310
Our school weather station logs a

2
00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.990
temperature every hour: twenty-four

3
00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:05.810
numbers a day.

4
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.030
What was the hottest hour?

5
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.620
How many hours were above thirty?

6
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.270
A list and a loop answer both.

7
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.870
Today: lists and loops together.

8
00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.290
A list keeps values in order.

9
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.590
Positions count from zero, so temps

10
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.650
at zero is the first reading.

11
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.700
Negative positions count from the end:

12
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.640
temps at minus one is the last.

13
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.850
len tells you how many there are.

14
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.520
append adds a reading to the end.

15
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.470
A slice takes part of a list: temps from

16
00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.410
one to three gives the items at one and

17
00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.310
two, stopping before three, just like

18
00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:48.570
range.

19
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.280
The list itself isn’t changed.

20
00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.910
Python has max, but writing it

21
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.290
yourself teaches the pattern.

22
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.500
Start with the first item

23
00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.860
as the best so far.

24
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.100
Loop over the rest, and whenever one

25
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.680
is bigger, keep it instead.

26
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.860
At the end, best is the largest.

27
00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.010
The same loop can count and total.

28
00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.270
Add one to a count whenever a

29
00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.690
reading passes a test.

30
00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.230
Add every reading to a total.

31
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.510
Then total divided by the

32
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.750
length is the average.

33
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.440
Three patterns, one loop.

34
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:27.190
Quick check!

35
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.910
The list is twenty-one,

36
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.070
twenty-five, thirty-one, twenty-eight.

37
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.350
What is temps at minus one?

38
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.460
Twenty-eight, the last reading.

39
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.780
And temps at four would stop the

40
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.260
program with an IndexError: four

41
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.840
items have positions zero to three.

42
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.000
Most list bugs are off by one.

43
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.360
A list can also act as a stack or a queue.

44
00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.780
append adds to the end.

45
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.230
pop takes from the end, last in, first

46
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.970
out: that’s a stack, like undo.

47
00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.450
pop of zero takes from the front, first

48
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.070
in, first out: that’s a queue.

49
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.900
Lists are everywhere in real programs.

50
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.800
A playlist is a list of songs.

51
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.090
A class register is a list of names.

52
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.540
A sensor log is a list of readings.

53
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.010
And your phone’s messages are

54
00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.870
a list, newest last.

55
00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.250
In the app, open Stacks and queues.

56
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.160
Type three words, make one bold,

57
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.300
then press Undo twice: a stack.

58
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.100
Then let four customers into the

59
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.880
queue and serve two: the first to

60
00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.220
arrive are served first.

61
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.040
Your turn.

62
00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.470
A loop adds one to hot whenever a

63
00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.990
reading is above thirty.

64
00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.630
For twenty-one, thirty-one,

65
00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.430
thirty-five and thirty, what is hot?

66
00:02:59.750 --> 00:02:59.950
Two.

67
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.640
Remember: positions count from

68
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.060
zero, and minus one is the last.

69
00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.630
A loop can find the largest,

70
00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.350
count and total in one pass.

71
00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.430
append adds; pop takes.

72
00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:16.350
And check the boundaries,

73
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.310
like exactly thirty.

74
00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.970
Try it yourself: for one week,

75
00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.030
write down how many minutes you

76
00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.690
spend on your phone each day.

77
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.430
Put the seven numbers in a list, and

78
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.550
write a program for the total, the

79
00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.390
average and the biggest day.
